course: dictionary
lesson: 5770
title: The lungs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The lungs are the two organs inside your chest that you breathe with. «When you breathe in, air goes down your windpipe into two lungs, which are full of tiny air bags.» In those air bags, oxygen passes into your blood.
explain: Breathing out gets rid of waste. «Carbon dioxide, a waste gas, passes out to be breathed away.» Your lungs take oxygen in and let carbon dioxide out, every time you breathe.
explain: Words can have meanings beyond the body. «He shouted the news at the top of his lungs.» Here the lungs are not the point; the phrase means very loudly, so use the clues around the word to work it out.
fact: The lungs are the two organs inside your chest that you breathe with.
fact: The lungs take oxygen from the air into your blood and let carbon dioxide out.
fact: The lungs are protected by the ribs, which curve round like a cage.
fact: The diaphragm is a muscle under the lungs that helps them fill and empty.
fact: «At the top of your lungs» means very loudly.
exercise: «When you breathe in, air goes down your windpipe into two lungs.» What do the lungs take from the air into your blood? | Dust | Heat | Sound | *Oxygen
exercise: «When you breathe in, a muscle under your lungs pulls down.» What does this muscle do? | It stops moving until you breathe out | *It pulls down, making your chest bigger | It turns into air | It pushes up and squeezes the lungs shut
exercise: «Twelve pairs of ribs curve round from the spine like a cage, guarding the heart and lungs.» Find and copy one word that means protecting. | cage | spine | *guarding | curve
test: «Tadpoles breathe with gills; during metamorphosis they grow lungs.» What do tadpoles use to breathe before they grow lungs? | Windpipes | Lungs | Ribs | *Gills
test: Leo shouted, «I'm telling everyone at the top of my lungs!» What does at the top of your lungs mean? | *Very loudly | Standing on a high place | Very slowly | Very quietly
exam: What is the main job of your lungs? | *Taking oxygen from the air into your blood and letting carbon dioxide out | Pumping blood round your body | Making your bones strong | Breaking down your food
